 the existing fixture, I can extend it or create tests that modify the seed.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is for the sort tests that need custom data, I can use the existing fixture to get the page, then make API calls to delete the default todos and create new ones with the specific properties I need. But the API doesn't let me set `created_at` directly, so I'd need to either use direct SQL with neon or find another way to control the timestamps.\n\nThe real issue is that the seed data inserts all 5 todos in a single SQL statement, which means they get essentially identical `created_at` timestamps. When the API orders by `created_at DESC`, if multiple todos have the same timestamp, the order becomes non-deterministic. I need to figure out how to either modify the fixture to stagger the creation times or use direct database access in the tests to set different timestamps.\n\nLooking at the actual test requirements, tests 3 and 4 need todos with explicitly different creation times, test 5 needs todos with different priorities, and test 6 needs todos with different due dates. So I'll need to either create separate fixtures for each test scenario or use a setup approach that allows me to insert data with specific timestamp values. I'm thinking the cleanest solution is to expose a `sql` fixture that gives each test direct database access, so they can truncate and reseed with their custom data after the standard setup runs. Writing test cases... Writing test assertions...\n\nActually, I realize the fixture handles the initial setup, so I can just run custom SQL before navigating if I need different seed data for specific tests. For the first couple tests the default data works fine, but test 3 will need its own dataset. Let me write out the full spec file with all the test cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1839,"cache_read_input_tokens":51303,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input